# Diagram sources and previews

[Package home](../README.md). The six architecture views have editable Mermaid sources and rendered SVG/PNG previews. The pool illustration is a metaphor; the architecture views define the boundaries. Color in the pool denotes compatibility with the current need. Colors in ownership diagrams distinguish responsibilities, not capsule types.

| View | Editable source | Preview |
|---|---|---|
| System ownership | [Mermaid](01-system.mmd) | [PNG](01-system.png) |
| Adaptive research example | [Mermaid](02-research-flow.mmd) | [PNG](02-research-flow.png) |
| Capabilities inside a capsule | [Mermaid](03-capsules.mmd) | [PNG](03-capsules.png) |
| Checking and pool quality | [Mermaid](04-reliability.mmd) | [PNG](04-reliability.png) |
| Artifact custody and restart | [Mermaid](05-artifacts.mmd) | [PNG](05-artifacts.png) |
| Incremental build and qualification | [Mermaid](06-build.mmd) | [PNG](06-build.png) |

Render edited sources with a Mermaid-compatible renderer, inspect the output, then refresh the source/output hashes in [diagram manifest](manifest.json). A successful parse is not enough: arrows must preserve the same acceptance and authority rules as the prose. In particular, recovered output returns to required checks before acceptance.

The [pool illustration](pool-illustration.png) was created in the earlier pool-metaphor discussion and copied unchanged into this design. It shows the agent outside the pool, matching and excluded floaties, bundled verification handles, admission, circulation and withdrawal.
